{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>An altar of stones.</b> Some say the reason is that they should not make engravings on the altar; therefore He commanded an altar of stones, because they are whole. Now these commentators — perhaps they will teach us why the entire Tabernacle was made with cherubim, and cherubim of gold upon the cover, and the house that Solomon built was all cherubim and figures and relief of rosettes (1 Kings 6:32), and these places are more honored than the place of the altar. In the section Tissa I will speak of this. Some say מזבח אדמה (altar of earth) means it should not be on a high place, but this is not the sense of the language. The proof is that it is written after it מזבח אבנים (altar of stones). Our predecessors said that the reason is to put earth in the bronze altar, and they spoke truth, for so they did, only this verse is for a memorial and as an asmakhta, and this is not its plain sense, for the earth they put in it is incidental, since it is called only the bronze altar, for it is made of bronze — and it is also called מזבח הקטרת (altar of incense), מזבח הזהב (altar of gold), because it is overlaid with gold. Furthermore, even if this were the sense, מזבח אדמה would be the interpretation of ואם מזבח אבנים (\"and if an altar of stones\") that they put stones in it, and we cannot say that the altar was not made of stones, for it is explicit, and it is what was made on Mount Ebal. Now I shall explain. The Gaon [Saadia] said that there is a dispute concerning the altar that Moses made at the foot of Mount Sinai: some say it was before the giving of the Torah, and others say after. The Gaon accepted the first position and said that the verse in this section, כל אשר דבר ה׳ נעשה (below 19:8), is repeated in the second section, כל אשר דבר ה׳ נעשה ונשמע (below 24:7). But I shall give you a decisive proof that it was after the Torah, for behold it is written in this section that HaShem said to Moses when he went up to the mountain of Elohim before the giving of the Torah: אתם ראיתם אשר עשיתי למצרים, ועתה אם שמוע תשמעו בקולי (above 19:4–5), and He said at the beginning כה תאמר לבית יעקב (ibid. v. 3). Now He did not declare to them neither commandments, nor statutes, nor laws, only to hear from Him whether they would be willing to obey the voice of HaShem in all that He would command them. And they answered and said נעשה (we will do). And there it is written וישב משה את דברי העם אל ה׳ (ibid. v. 8). Then He sanctified them to hear the word of HaShem, which was the Ten Commandments. And now He began to make them hear His commandments. And when Moses drew near to the cloud, He said to him this section, לא תעשון (you shall not make), and the entire section of ואלה המשפטים (and these are the laws) until כי יהיה לך למוקש (below 23:33). And after that it is written ויבא משה (ibid. 24:3) that he came down from the mountain, and there it is written: ויספר לעם את כל דברי ה׳ ואת כל המשפטים (ibid.). Now דבר ה׳ (the word of YHWH) are positive and negative commandments, whose beginning is לא תעשון אתי אלהי כסף ואלהי זהב (you shall not make with Me gods of silver and gods of gold), and the matter of the altar, and every positive and negative commandment that is in the section ואלה המשפטים, such as השב תשיבנו לו (you shall surely return it to him) until בא השמש תשיבנו לו (ibid. 22:25), אלהים לא תקלל (ibid. v. 27). And the משפטים (laws) are the matters of slaves, and those who strike, and the fighters, and goring, and burning, and the matters of theft, and oath. And concerning these it is written there ואת כל המשפטים (and all the laws). And because of this our ancestors said נעשה ונשמע (below 24:7), meaning: נעשה (we will do) — to keep positive and negative commandments, and נשמע (we will hear) — concerning the laws; therefore after the giving of the Torah they added to say ונשמע, which they did not say at first. Now behold, the meaning of מזבח אדמה is as it sounds: made of earth. And this is what is written there: ויבן מזבח תחת ההר (ibid. ibid. 4). And it is written here וזבחת עליו את עלתיך ואת שלמיך (and you shall sacrifice upon it your burnt offerings and your peace offerings), and there it is written ויעלו עולות ויזבחו זבחים שלמים (ibid. ibid. 5). And there is no doubt that this is the section of מזבח אדמה, also לא תעשון אתי (you shall not make with Me) — on account of the blood of the covenant that he sprinkled upon the altar and upon the people, who accepted upon themselves that HaShem alone would be their Elohim, and that they would keep His commandments and His laws. And He mentioned את צאנך ואת בקרך (your sheep and your cattle), for both are kinds of burnt offerings and peace offerings. I saw one heretic — may his bones be ground — who attacked the Sages because they said: which is the place of slaughter? (Zevaḥim 14a). For he said that he did not find in all the Torah slaughter except for peace offerings, for עולה (burnt offering) is a thing unto itself, and זבחים (slaughtered offerings) are peace offerings, as in ויעלו עולות ויזבחו זבחים שלמים לה׳ פרים (below 24:5), and so everywhere. And I showed him that he did not speak correctly, for it is written וזבחת עליו את עלתיך ואת שלמיך (and you shall sacrifice upon it your burnt offerings and your peace offerings). Then he confessed his sin, that he had attacked people more distinguished than all the generations that came after them." "And the meaning of בכל המקום אשר אזכיר את שמי (in every place where I mention My name): in every place where I set a remembrance for My name, there My honor dwells, such as Shiloh and Nov, where the Ark stood. If you come to the place mentioned for My name to visit Me, I too will come to you and bless you." "And the meaning of אבוא (I will come) is in the manner of a human being. And the meaning is: you will find us there, in the place where I mention My name upon it, and it is the place of the Glory, as though I came to you — knowing that you will come, and for your sake I came. And because you came for My honor, I will come to you and bless you, and you will not need another, for you will find Him at every time that His honor is there."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {0 "The citation זבחים מז א resolved as Zevaḥim 14a based on the standard locution of the passage in the Talmud."}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:36:51.195420516Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}